; fault_unpredicated.kasm -- a decode-detected fault ignores the predicate.
;
; The counterpart to fault_squashed.kasm, and the line between them is where
; the fault was detected rather than how bad it is:
;
;   * Execute-time faults (misaligned, out of range, bad port) depend on a
;     register value, so a squashed instruction never computes one and never
;     faults.
;   * Decode-time faults (DIV, LDF32, LLC_F32, BEXT with a zero span, an
;     unassigned opcode, an illegal prefix combination) are properties of the
;     encoding. An encoding that cannot be executed is rejected whether or not
;     this pass over it would have run.
;
; This program pins the second rule, which the two machines used to disagree
; about: the RTL faulted here and the emulator ran on to the halt. The cause
; was `uop_fault` in k2g_types.svh clearing the accumulated prefix state --
; including the predicate -- so `cond_met` was forced true for every decode
; fault. Rather than teach the hardware to carry a predicate into its fault
; path, the rule is now spelled out in spec 8 and the emulator follows it.
; expect: FAULT_DIV_UNIMPLEMENTED

        .org    0

        puc32   r1, 8
        puc32   r2, 2
        puc32   r3, 0
        div     r1, r2 if !r3.zero  ; squashed, and still DIV_UNIMPLEMENTED
        halt
